    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [X] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [X] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.pairs\n\n### Description\n\nI couldn't find an issue or discussion for this. How do you approach situations where you don't want auto pairing to get in the way, just temporarily? I feel like I'm missing something.\r\n\r\n**Use Case 1:**\r\nLets say I have a block of Lua code I want to comment out and I want to do it by hand.\r\nWhen using `mini.pairs`, it's kinda frustrating to type `--[==[` on a line because I get `--[==[]]`.\r\n\r\n**Use Case 2:**\r\nI realize I'm missing a quote on a word and just want to insert a single quote at the beginning/end.\r\nBut each time, the second quote is inserted, and removed when I backspace.\r\n\r\n**Available Solutions:**\r\n1. Use `<C-V>` before typing each character\r\n2. Use DEL key after typing each character\r\n3. Exit insert mode, and delete extra characters\r\n\r\n**Suggestion:**\r\nWould it make sense to provide a binding that for example disables autopair until leaving insert mode?",
